          "id": "ebfc417d26d243962263cf38060326114795bc01",
          "message": "perf(decode): fold the graph's padded tail into the deferred-id kernel (#2368)\n\nA decode step wrote `input_ids` in two launches: one Triton kernel filling\neach carried-over request's span, then a `zero_()` over the rows a replayed\ngraph reads but the batch never scheduled. The second is a 5.6us dispatch\nfloor for ~1.5KB, and it fires on every step -- `bs` sits between two capture\nbuckets on all but the exact-fit ones.\n\nThe two regions are disjoint by construction: the tail starts where the last\nrequest ends, so nothing orders them and one launch can do both. Programs past\n`num_requests` zero `[cu[bs], width)` instead of filling a span.\n\n`cu` stays the only statement of where requests end -- the padding programs\nread `cu[bs]` themselves rather than taking a second copy of it from the host,\nso the two cannot disagree. `width` is a required argument, because a caller\nthat forgets it leaves the padded rows carrying the previous step's ids, and\nthe MoE path does consume them.\n\n`BLOCK_PAD` is one value so the tail costs one JIT specialization rather than\none per capture bucket, and the three new runtime ints are `do_not_specialize`\nfor the same reason.\n\nVerified: GSM8K 1319 @ 3-shot on DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash DSpark-5, TP4, bf16 KV,\nindex fp8 -- flexible 0.9174 / strict 0.9166 against a 0.9224 baseline mean\n(sigma ~0.005). Unit suite 8512 passed, unchanged from before the change.",
          "timestamp": "2026-09-23T14:59:51+08:00",
          "tree_id": "c64b05c3e0b9cbebb3d3342375058ca185671a4c",
