          "distinct": true,
          "id": "c8f7f0583c07e5d3cf77ab01a44f2fce860e7835",
          "message": "feat(kimi-k3): E8M0 block scales for online per_1x128 FP8 on gfx1250 (#2554)\n\n* feat(kimi-k3): E8M0 block scales for online per_1x128 FP8 on gfx1250\n\nOnline per_block_fp8 re-quantizes BF16 layers to per_1x128, but the Linear\ndecided blockscale_e8m0_scale from the checkpoint scheme (No for a BF16\nlayer), so the weight kept FP32 128x128 scales and the activation was\nquantized with FP32 scales too. On gfx1250 aiter's\ngemm_a8w8_blockscale_bpreshuffle takes the mxfp8_128 (FlyDSL / tuned\nTriton) path only when both operand scales are E8M0; anything else falls\nto the generic Triton blockscale GEMM.\n\n- quantize_weight_to_fp8_128x128_blockscale(e8m0_scale=True): round each\n  block scale up to a power of two, re-quantize with it, store E8M0\n  (exact, no clipping). quant_weight_online threads it through.\n- LinearBase.online_quantize_weight decides blockscale_e8m0_scale from the\n  online target (per_1x128 and QuantizationConfig.blockscale_e8m0_scale)\n  and stores it, so the activation quant follows.\n- Online-produced E8M0 per_1x128 weights with N % 128 != 0 are padded to\n  128 rows like the per_Token a8w8 path (the mxfp8_128 FlyDSL kernels need\n  N % 128 == 0); the block scale already has ceil(N/128) rows.\n  Checkpoint-native E8M0 layers are not padded. supports_out() is False\n  for a padded layer.\n- Kimi-K3: the q_a norm+quant and sigmoid-mul+quant producers, which only\n  emit FP32 block scales, stand down for an E8M0 consumer; KDA o_norm\n  fuses only per-token FP8 and no longer asserts when o_proj is per_1x128\n  (it emits bf16 and o_proj quantizes its own activation).\n\nBehavior changes only where QuantizationConfig.blockscale_e8m0_scale is\nTrue for an online per_1x128 target (ATOM_FP8_BLOCKSCALE_USE_E8M0_SCALE=1,\nor a checkpoint declaring scale_fmt ue8m0 on a non-gfx942 GPU), plus the\nKDA o_norm fix for any per_1x128 o_proj.\n\n* fix(kimi-k3): requantize padded E8M0 weights on rollout sync\n\nOnline E8M0 per_1x128 pads N to a multiple of 128, so a later BF16\nweight sync no longer matched the stored parameter and was dropped.\nPad back to the stored N and emit E8M0 scales, matching the load path.\n\nCo-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>\n\n* refactor(linear): carry a scale_dtype instead of E8M0 flags\n\nA layer's scale format is now one attribute, LinearBase.scale_dtype,\nresolved by _quant_scale_dtype() from the quant scheme: E8M0 for MX\n(per_1x32), E8M0 or FP32 for 128x128 blocks as\nQuantizationConfig.blockscale_e8m0_scale says, FP32 otherwise. __init__\nand online_quantize_weight both set it, so the online target no longer\nrepeats the per_1x128 test. blockscale_e8m0_scale stays as a read-only\nproperty for its readers.\n\nquant_weight_online and quantize_weight_to_fp8_128x128_blockscale take\nscale_dtype instead of a bool; the rollout requantize passes the stored\nscale's dtype.\n\nThe E8M0 output pad is limited to gfx1250, whose mxfp8_128 GEMM needs\nN % 128 == 0, so other archs keep main's layout.\n\n* test(e8m0): expect the N pad only where it applies\n\nThe E8M0 output pad runs on gfx1250 only, so the rollout test that also\nruns the GEMM now expects N padded on gfx1250 and unpadded elsewhere\n(e.g. gfx950). The two tests that check the pad itself on CPU pin the\narch to gfx1250 and run the same on any machine.\n\n* fix(rollout): keep main's FP32 requantize for checkpoint scales\n\nOnly online quant builds an E8M0 scale that the rollout requantize has\nto rebuild the same way. A checkpoint that ships E8M0 block scales\n(DeepSeek-V4) keeps main's path: FP32 scale, cast into the scale buffer.\n\n* fix(linear): pad E8M0 128x128 weights on every arch\n\ngfx950's E8M0 GEMM path reads the w_scale block as N // scale rows and\naccepts only 32 or 128 (aiter mxscale_w_scale_block), so an unpadded\nN % 128 != 0 fails there as well: on MI355, N = 2112 with 17 scale rows\nraises in mxscale_w_scale_block. Drop the gfx1250-only gate. Checkpoint\nE8M0 layers that already run on main have N % 128 == 0, so the pad is a\nno-op for them.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: xytpai <xytpai@foxmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>",
